9, 11 give a constant second difference of 2, so $a = 1$. Solving $1 + b + c = 2$ and $4 + 2b + c = 7$ gives $b = 2$, $c = -1$, so $u_n = n^2 + 2n - 1$.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Quadratic sequences","st":"general term from differences","cfg":{"question_number":14,"paper_question_key":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"In an arithmetic sequence, $u_4 = 17$ and $u_{10} = 41$. Calculate $u_{25}$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In an arithmetic sequence, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_4 = 17"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_{10} = 41"}},{"type":"text","text":". Calculate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_{25}"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"$d = \\frac{41 - 17}{10 - 4} = 4$ and $u_1 = 17 - 3 \\times 4 = 5$, so $u_{25} = 5 + 24 \\times 4 = 101$.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic sequences","st":"finding a distant term from two terms","cfg":{"question_number":15,"paper_question_key":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"A geometric sequence of positive terms has $u_2 = 12$ and $u_5 = 324$. Find $u_1$ and $r$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence of positive terms has "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_2 = 12"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_5 = 324"}},{"type":"text","text":". Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"$r^3 = \\frac{324}{12} = 27$, so $r = 3$ and $u_1 = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric sequences","st":"first term and ratio from two terms","cfg":{"question_number":16,"paper_question_key":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"A tapered wall has 35 bricks in Row 1, decreasing by 3 bricks per row up to 11 bricks in Row 9. Find the total bricks in all 9 rows.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A tapered wall has 35 bricks in Row 1, decreasing by 3 bricks per row up to 11 bricks in Row 9. Find the total bricks in all 9 rows."}]}]},"e":"$S_9 = \\frac{9}{2}(35 + 11) = \\frac{9}{2} \\times 46 = 207$ bricks.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic series in context","st":"sum of a decreasing arithmetic series","cfg":{"question_number":17,"paper_question_key":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"Find the sum $S_7$ of the first 7 terms of the geometric series 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + …","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Find the sum "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_7"}},{"type":"text","text":" of the first 7 terms of the geometric series 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + …"}]}]},"e":"With $a = 3$ and $r = 2$: $S_7 = \\frac{3(2^7 - 1)}{2 - 1} = 3 \\times 127 = 381$.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric series","st":"sum of the first n terms","cfg":{"question_number":18,"paper_question_key":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"A solar array generates 18 kWh on Day 1 and increases by 2.5 kWh each day. On which day does daily output equal 53 kWh?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A solar array generates 18 kWh on Day 1 and increases by 2.5 kWh each day. On which day does daily output equal 53 kWh?"}]}]},"e":"$18 + 2.5(n - 1) = 53 \\Rightarrow 2.5(n - 1) = 35 \\Rightarrow n - 1 = 14 \\Rightarrow n = 15$.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic sequences in context","st":"solving for the term number","cfg":{"question_number":19,"paper_question_key":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":20,"t":"single_choice","p":"Water enters a purifier at 500 mg/L. Each filter pass removes 20 % of remaining impurities ($u_1 = 400$ mg/L after 1 pass). Find $u_3$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Water enters a purifier at 500 mg/L. Each filter pass removes 20 % of remaining impurities ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1 = 400"}},{"type":"text","text":" mg/L after 1 pass). Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_3"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"Each pass keeps 80 %, so $r = 0.8$ and $u_n = 400(0.8)^{n-1}$. Hence $u_3 = 400(0.8)^2 = 256$ mg/L.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric sequences in context","st":"repeated percentage decay","cfg":{"question_number":20,"paper_question_key":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"Consecutive terms $k - 1$, $k + 3$, $3k + 1$ form a geometric sequence with $r > 1$. Find $k$ and $r$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consecutive terms "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"k - 1"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"k + 3"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"3k + 1"}},{"type":"text","text":" form a geometric sequence with "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r > 1"}},{"type":"text","text":". Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"k"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"$\\frac{k+3}{k-1} = \\frac{3k+1}{k+3} \\Rightarrow (k+3)^2 = (k-1)(3k+1) \\Rightarrow k^2 - 4k - 5 = 0$, so $k = 5$ or $k = -1$. With $r > 1$, $k = 5$ gives the terms 4, 8, 16 and $r = 2$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Geometric sequences","st":"solving for an unknown term in a geometric sequence","cfg":{"question_number":21,"paper_question_key":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"An amphitheatre has 24 seats in Row 1, 28 in Row 2, 32 in Row 3, and so on. How many rows give a total capacity of 780 seats?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An amphitheatre has 24 seats in Row 1, 28 in Row 2, 32 in Row 3, and so on. How many rows give a total capacity of 780 seats?"}]}]},"e":"With $a = 24$ and $d = 4$: $S_n = \\frac{n}{2}(48 + 4(n-1)) = 2n^2 + 22n = 780$, so $n^2 + 11n - 390 = 0$ and $(n + 26)(n - 15) = 0$, giving $n = 15$ rows.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic series in context","st":"solving for the number of terms","cfg":{"question_number":22,"paper_question_key":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":23,"t":"single_choice","p":"Evaluate the exact value of the finite geometric series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{6} 243\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{k-1}$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Evaluate the exact value of the finite geometric series "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{6} 243\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{k-1}"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"With $a = 243$, $r = \\frac{2}{3}$ and $n = 6$: $S_6 = \\frac{243\\left(1 - (\\frac{2}{3})^6\\right)}{1 - \\frac{2}{3}} = 729\\left(1 - \\frac{64}{729}\\right) = 729 - 64 = 665$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Geometric series","st":"sigma notation and exact sums","cfg":{"question_number":23,"paper_question_key":23,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":24,"t":"single_choice","p":"A sequence satisfies $u_1 = 4$ and $u_{n+1} = u_n + 2n + 3$ for $n \\ge 1$. Which explicit quadratic formula defines $u_n$?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A sequence satisfies "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1 = 4"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_{n+1} = u_n + 2n + 3"}},{"type":"text","text":" for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n \\ge 1"}},{"type":"text","text":". Which explicit quadratic formula defines "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n"}},{"type":"text","text":"?"}]}]},"e":"$u_2 = 4 + 5 = 9$, $u_3 = 9 + 7 = 16$, $u_4 = 16 + 9 = 25$, giving 4, 9, 16, 25 = $(n + 1)^2 = n^2 + 2n + 1$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Recursive rules","st":"recursive rule to explicit quadratic formula","cfg":{"question_number":24,"paper_question_key":24,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":25,"t":"single_choice","p":"Plan A saves 10 000 FCFA in Month 1, adding 5 000 FCFA each month. Plan B saves 2 000 FCFA in Month 1, doubling each month. Compare 6-month totals ($S_6$).","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Plan A saves 10 000 FCFA in Month 1, adding 5 000 FCFA each month. Plan B saves 2 000 FCFA in Month 1, doubling each month. Compare 6-month totals ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_6"}},{"type":"text","text":")."}]}]},"e":"Plan A: $S_6 = \\frac{6}{2}(10\\,000 + 35\\,000) = 135\\,000$ FCFA. Plan B: $S_6 = \\frac{2\\,000(2^6 - 1)}{2 - 1} = 126\\,000$ FCFA. Plan A exceeds Plan B by 9 000 FCFA.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Comparing series in context","st":"arithmetic vs geometric savings","cfg":{"question_number":25,"paper_question_key":25,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-sequences-and-series-summative-2026"}},{"n":26,"t":"open_ended","p":"Explicit & recursive term generation [3 marks]\n(a) Sequence A has explicit rule $u_n = 3n^2 - 2n + 4$. Calculate $u_1$, $u_3$ and $u_8$. [1]\n(b) Sequence B satisfies $v_1 = 6$ and $v_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{2}v_n + 5$ $(n \\ge 1)$. Generate $v_2$, $v_3$ and $v_4$, showing each step. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit & recursive term generation [3 marks]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Sequence A has explicit rule "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n = 3n^2 - 2n + 4"}},{"type":"text","text":". Calculate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_3"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_8"}},{"type":"text","text":". [1]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Sequence B satisfies "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_1 = 6"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{2}v_n + 5"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(n \\ge 1)"}},{"type":"text","text":". Generate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_2"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"t